  Status: draft; bounded mathematical/source pass requested, not completed.

  <section|Source and scope><label|source-and-scope>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Imported Lean source pin from <verbatim|source/upstream.json> and
    <verbatim|source/README.md>: <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.
    The enclosing prose repository checkout at inspection was
    <verbatim|ecc06d13de67e479666203061918e537316994e3>. The source pin is a
    manifest value; it is not expected to exist as an object in the prose
    repository's Git database. The inspected Lean files are the imported
    <verbatim|Lean/> tree at that manifest pin.

    <item>This unit follows the public selected route from
    <verbatim|YM.Stationary.geared> through <verbatim|under_contract>,
    <verbatim|pouring>, <verbatim|flux_cap>, <verbatim|Weather.mastering>,
    <verbatim|Weather.florentine>, <verbatim|MailBox.settled>, and
    <verbatim|Train.Miracles.relayT>.

    <item>Manuscript output: <verbatim|manuscript/existence/path-attachment-producer.tex>.

    <item>Scope: expand the exact annulus interface and its selected
    <verbatim|pouring> producer, the radial logarithmic coefficient estimate,
    the one-step flux interface/provenance, finite adjacent-scale
    unification, finite-horizon descent, and positive-root cutoff
    convergence.

    <item>This does not claim to reproduce the full proof of
    <verbatim|flux_cap> from every internal response/curvature receipt. The
    <verbatim|Scud> input itself is stated exactly; its <verbatim|PriceGap>,
    <verbatim|Critical>, <verbatim|Guessed>, and <verbatim|HeatG> parts are
    produced by the fixed-volume stationary-scene chain expanded below. The
    funded trial, armada/bed bounds, and selected local response tickets
    remain separate inputs.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Inspected declarations><label|inspected-declarations>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.HorizCalc>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean>: quantifies
    first over all sufficiently large odd annulus sides, then over every
    cofinal mesh sequence, bed, clip, path, and budget. Inputs are
    <verbatim|PathData.Bounds>, <verbatim|Attached>, the metric strip, direct
    clip bound, all floors, and all sworn conditions. Outputs are one
    analytic/firm picture, one error sequence tending to zero, the recorded
    <verbatim|PoleTest> identity at each mesh, and a uniform colorwise
    logarithmic coefficient estimate.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.pouring>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/FullPlanned.lean>: applies
    <verbatim|OneLoop.Lattice.vintageWilson>,
    <verbatim|OneLoop.Lattice.saunter>, <verbatim|OneLoop.Bubbles.astronomy>,
    <verbatim|HouseWord.done>, <verbatim|profit>, and
    <verbatim|OneLoop.MomentumIntegral.absoluteGeode> in that order.

    <item><verbatim|vintageWilson> and <verbatim|saunter>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Lattice.lean>: Wilson orders
    one through three are fee-controlled by <verbatim|Wilson_stream> and
    <verbatim|jig>; the second Jar binds <verbatim|intended p R - WilsonQ>
    under the row, floor, attachment, and strip assumptions. The combined Jar
    binds the intended action. The source then applies
    <verbatim|Jar.cast_book>, <verbatim|gemF>, <verbatim|massifTabs>, and the
    HouseWord/profit construction.

    <item><verbatim|astronomy>, <verbatim|Waves1>, and <verbatim|Waves2>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/Bubbles.lean>: for each
    admissible local perturbation and scale <verbatim|mu>, the productive
    momentum density has bounds <verbatim|A/(\|k\|+mu)^4> and, on
    <verbatim|mu\<leq\>\|k\|\<leq\>1>, pole error <verbatim|D/\|k\|^3 +
    D*mu^2/\|k\|^6>.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.absoluteGeode>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/MomentumIntegral.lean>: proves
    the logarithmic coefficient estimate by polar integration and splitting
    the integration region into the ball of radius <verbatim|mu>, the
    annulus, and the exterior of the unit ball. <verbatim|polar_clock>,
    <verbatim|poleSpin>, <verbatim|polar_m>, and <verbatim|peel_p> provide
    the angular pole identity, exact logarithmic integral, and two annular
    integrals.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.Scud>, <verbatim|YM.Precision.Critical>,
    <verbatim|YM.Precision.Guessed>, <verbatim|YM.Precision.PriceGap>, and
    <verbatim|YM.Precision.ClassicalMatch>, in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean> and
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean>: <verbatim|Scud> takes the price gap,
    critical chart, guessed chart, heat, and trial receipts as inputs. They
    are not produced by <verbatim|flux_cap>. <verbatim|Critical> contains a
    vanishing smooth chart and local stationarity of the tested action;
    <verbatim|Guessed> specifies the derivative at zero from the installed
    linear chart; <verbatim|PriceGap> is the uniform positive Hessian lower
    bound. <verbatim|ClassicalMatch> is the equality of all Taylor jets of
    orders at most four between the tested action along the critical chart
    and the new path's main term.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.flux_cap>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Canvas/Flux.lean>: consumes
    <verbatim|HorizCalc> and a <verbatim|Scud> package, then constructs a
    path with the exact <verbatim|Fluxed> conclusion. Its proof uses
    <verbatim|honored>, <verbatim|setWest>, <verbatim|pierce>,
    <verbatim|tideMark>, <verbatim|bequest>, <verbatim|color_pipe>,
    <verbatim|quoting>, <verbatim|pole_reduced>, <verbatim|age_control>, and
    a final <verbatim|rampInvoice>/<verbatim|winter_exp> comparison. The
    scalar normalization is selected as a local <verbatim|alphaPay> value;
    the residual from the annulus coefficient is bounded by the response
    ticket and added to the <verbatim|HorizCalc> bound by the triangle
    inequality.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.scene_gate>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Canvas/SliceGate.lean>, obtains the
    <verbatim|PriceGap>, <verbatim|Critical>, and <verbatim|Guessed> receipts
    from <verbatim|Integrated.stationary_scene>, which is the exact upstream
    source <verbatim|Bid.cospons Work.E02.answer Work.F01.answer ...
    Work.F04.answer> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Integrated.lean>. The manuscript
    now expands this local producer. <verbatim|Work.E02.answer> chooses the
    planted constants and proves the edge bound by a root walk, the two
    endpoint frame costs on a crossing edge, and the predictor wire. The
    double finite Cauchy--Schwarz sum and the bed floor yield the Hessian
    gap. <verbatim|Work.F01.answer> sets <verbatim|eta=min(a,penalty)/(1+curlFactor+probeFactor)>
    so both costs fit the finite-volume Hessian. <verbatim|Work.F02.answer>
    obtains smoothness of the uncut main action from the complex analytic
    row/atom series and their weighted absolute convergence.
    <verbatim|Work.F03.answer> identifies the forest-chart action germ with
    the flat action, then differentiates to transfer its Hessian.
    <verbatim|Work.F04.answer> proves vertical Hessian invertibility from the
    price gap, applies the finite-dimensional implicit-function theorem,
    differentiates stationarity to obtain the installed linear chart, and
    uses the two-scale diagonal identity for the heat/Hessian equality.

    <item><verbatim|YM.Stationary.mastering>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Canvas/Weather.lean>, applies
    <verbatim|flux_cap> at both adjacent schedule indices. It takes the
    minimum width and maximum path budget across the current and next index.
    <verbatim|Weather.florentine> builds each bubble/wing using the
    corresponding <verbatim|Fluxed> object and <verbatim|transfer_bound>.
    <verbatim|MailBox.settled>, <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Canvas/Mails.lean>,
    performs finite descending induction separately at each terminal cutoff;
    there is no compactness extraction at this step.

    <item><verbatim|Train.Miracles.relayT>,
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Train/Miracles.lean>, obtains retained tower
    limits from <verbatim|Bid.pinsT>; the positive-root uniqueness argument
    is finite-dimensional. For symmetric positive semidefinite matrices, the
    positive square root of a fixed positive semidefinite limit is unique.
    Uniform entry bounds give subsequential compactness, and uniqueness of
    cluster points gives convergence.
  </enumerate>

  <section|Mathematical steps expanded><label|mathematical-steps-expanded>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The radial lemma is stated with the necessary region,
    integrability, and coefficient hypotheses: a finite measurable region
    <verbatim|E_4> containing the unit ball, real integrable <verbatim|g>,
    integrable pole <verbatim|h_G>, positive <verbatim|c_wt>,
    <verbatim|alphaPay=c_wt\<big-int\>g>, and
    <verbatim|c_wt\<big-int\>h_G=-slopeConstant*log(mu^-1)>. The last
    identity is proved by <verbatim|poleSpin> from <verbatim|polar_m> and
    <verbatim|joy_two/joy_heights>; the coefficient identity is
    <verbatim|polar_clock>. The proof then follows from the exact
    <verbatim|Waves1/Waves2> inequalities. <verbatim|vol(B_r)=pi^2*r^4/2>
    gives the inner-ball contribution <verbatim|A*pi^2/2>. On the exterior,
    <verbatim|\|k\|\<gtr\>=1> gives the contribution at most
    <verbatim|A*vol(E_4)>. On the annulus, <verbatim|int \|k\|^-3 =
    2*pi^2*(1-mu)> and <verbatim|int \|k\|^-6 = pi^2*(mu^-2-1)>; after
    multiplying the latter by <verbatim|mu^2>, their total is at most
    <verbatim|3*pi^2*D>. The exact pole integral is
    <verbatim|-slopeConstant*log(mu^-1)>. This proves the displayed constant
    <verbatim|costZone(A,D)> without suppressing the inner or outer regions.

    <item>The final ramp budget is the source inequality with its exact
    assumptions <verbatim|Lambda/128\<leq\>Lambda'> and
    <verbatim|B*gamma\<leq\>1/256>: <verbatim|(C + \|x\| V + B Lambda)*gamma
    \<less\>= Lambda'>. From <verbatim|\|x+y\|+1\<less\>=d>, derive
    <verbatim|\|x\|\<less\>=d+\|y\|>; charge the first terms using
    <verbatim|2*(C+(d+\|y\|)V)*gamma\<less\>=Lambda'>, and the annulus width
    term using <verbatim|B*gamma\<less\>=1/256> and
    <verbatim|Lambda/128\<less\>=Lambda'>.

    <item>The finite history proof is ordinary downward induction for each
    fixed cutoff, then stores each finite column in one horizon. No diagonal
    limit or unproved compatibility between independently chosen cutoffs is
    used.

    <item>For the positive-root step, any convergent subsequence of bounded
    positive-root matrices has a positive semidefinite limit whose square is
    the prescribed limit matrix. Uniqueness of that positive square root
    forces all cluster points to coincide; finite-dimensional boundedness
    then implies convergence of the complete sequence.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Remaining producer obligations><label|remaining-producer-obligations>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Expand or cite a separately reviewed proof for the internal
    quantitative receipts in <verbatim|flux_cap> (<verbatim|honored>,
    <verbatim|setWest>, <verbatim|pierce>, <verbatim|tideMark>,
    <verbatim|bequest>, <verbatim|color_pipe>, <verbatim|quoting>,
    <verbatim|pole_reduced>, and the local response tickets). Their names
    alone are not a proof of the complete one-step theorem.

    <item>The manuscript's finite-cutoff passage now states the exact
    retained-horizon output and proves cover-row recovery from convergence of
    every physical period action using <verbatim|bank_injective>. Remaining
    tower components are obtained from committed finite matrix product
    formulas; reviewer should confirm this gives enough detail for each
    component of <verbatim|deliver>.

    <item><verbatim|MailBox.settled> assembles finite-cutoff
    <verbatim|FullPaths>; the common selected coefficientwise path limit and
    larger-width <verbatim|Cadre.evolution> step are outside this unit and
    must be supplied by the parent unit before any continuum path conclusion.

    <item>Check the source-declared <verbatim|Odd> convention and the
    dependence of <verbatim|HorizCalc> constants on
    <verbatim|sigma,epsilon,C,Z,tau,A> when integrating with adjacent
    chapters.
  </itemize-minus>

